</h2><p style=3D"display:block;color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,seri=
f;margin:0 0 10px"><span>By Somini Sengupta</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Instant noodle=
s. Hot chapatis. Sliced bread. Wheat is at the heart of the modern diet. Bu=
t this year, the breadbaskets of the world are in trouble.</span></p><p sty=
le=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span=
>Heat and drought have diminished harvests in some of the biggest wheat-pro=
ducing countries. What little wheat is being harvested is now more expensiv=
e to transport, thanks to war and weather.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The simultaneou=
s troubles reveal the risks inherent in the global food system today. Only =
a few crops, like wheat, provide most of the world=E2=80=99s calories. Rela=
tively few governments dominate the export of wheat, including Russia, Cana=
da, the United States, Ukraine and the European Union. Their customers are =
mainly low-income countries that no longer grow much of the food their peop=
le eat.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#33=
3;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CThe system we currently have in place dep=
ends on three things: seamless trade, cheap energy, stable climate,=E2=80=
=9D said Evan Fraser, a professor who studies the global food system at the=
 University of Guelph in Ontario. =E2=80=9CToday, it seems none of these as=
sumptions are met.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geo=
rgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">War,=
 weather and wheat</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Russia and Ukraine together account for=
 nearly a third of the wheat that the world buys. When Russia invaded Ukrai=
ne and blocked its Black Sea grain trade in 2022 at the beginning of their =
war, the sharp increase in global food prices put tens of millions of peopl=
e at increased risk of acute food insecurity.</span></p><p style=3D"font:no=
rmal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The grain ev=
entually started flowing again, and prices fell from their peak. But this y=
ear, just as harvests began, Russian and Ukrainian grain shipments have onc=
e again nearly ground to a halt.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25p=
x georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In recent weeks, both Rus=
sia and Ukraine have stepped up their attacks on each other=E2=80=99s grain=
 export terminals and ships in the Black Sea. In the critical Russian Black=
 Sea port city of Novorossiysk, grain terminals suspended operations after =
a drone attack this month.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That attack followed </span><a =
href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/hLgoHAcyBbCF8hg6ctF2Eg~~/AAAAAR=
A~/p8PXcO9us-hRTlGz1PZ2lomvWwx_dbByeYPK6GCvv02S4n9Xcs2jgqFQ9MlbDB3wQXFQi9su=
vrV6uiQHdhm0OnL9t4fykN6vX3T5dDxWZVV9SCYzocOjTrnS5I1sK4z2-QmAapGimJK-xSczFHS=
