rgin:0 0 15px"><span>In other years, the world might have been able to rely=
 on European wheat producers to pick up some of the slack. But not this yea=
r.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;mar=
gin:0 0 15px"><span>France, which is the E.U.=E2=80=99s largest wheat produ=
cer, expects lower yields because of record heat this summer, according to =
government projections. Germany=E2=80=99s main farmers group said on Tuesda=
y that yields were down for several crops, including wheat. In July, the Eu=
ropean trade group for agricultural commodities, Coceral, issued a special =
bulletin, estimating lower grain yields of 286.6 million metric tons this y=
ear, compared with 310 million metric tons in 2025, for the continent and f=
or Britain.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color=
:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Australia, also a major wheat exporter, plante=
d fewer acres of wheat because of high fertilizer costs, stemming from the =
Strait of Hormuz closure. The U.S. is about to have its worst wheat harvest=
 since 1970, largely because of a terrible drought in the Great Plains.</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">A wake-up call</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>F=
ew things are as politically potent as the price of bread. Wheat prices wer=
e already at a three-year high in July, according to the Food and Agricultu=
re Organization. The rising prices will put enormous pressure on government=
s that are forced to buy high and then help subsidize the costs to keep bre=
ad prices within reach.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia=
,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Egypt, which buys most of its whea=
t from abroad, including from Russia and Ukraine, has tried to bolster whea=
t production at home, with the government offering higher prices to farmers=
=2E Still, Egypt remains far from reaching anything close to self-sufficien=
cy. There is simply not enough water to produce what the country needs.</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span>Then there are President Trump=E2=80=99s tariffs. To try to a=
void additional higher rates, some wheat-importing emerging economies, such=
